ven if the results were similar to the above examples.<br /> <br /> The only difference here is that the Democrats in all those elections were much, much stronger in the Congress before the presidential elections, so despite similar (or worse) swings the government remained divided. The only thing we haven't seen from the Republicans in 100 years was getting control across the federal board (though we have seen it multiple times in the past 100 years for the Democrats).<br /> <br /> Those were historic blow out elections.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Again, you keep asserting that the marginalized motivation for Trump supporters was specifically anti-Democratic. What's your evidence on this? Everything I've seen suggests that it was not motivated by specific party positions and policies over general dissatisfaction with the establishment. The Parties of the nominees was incidental to the motivation of why they voted.<br /> <br /> Also, the Democrats can't rely on unions, because the Republicans have union busted so hard that unions aren't strong enough to carry that voter bloc any more. What remains of the industrial unions in the Rust Belt is a shadow of their former power and influence. All of the strong unions that can really pull in votes are ones like teacher and service unions which overlap more with urban population centers anyway. <br /> <br /> Can we stop asserting that the Democrats lost these votes purely by apathy or laziness? There's decades of active political attacks on the institutions the Democrats relied on to organize those votes by the Republicans (because hey, that's part of politics, divide and conquer the other side's voting blocs) that have been trying to pry at those very voters' loyalty to the Democrats.<br /> <br /> It's one of the reasons I'm continually frustrated by this whole, &amp;quot;Trump is liberals fault!&amp;quot; thing. It ignores decades of attempts by the Republican party and conservative activists to make this happen to try to open the space for another Regan. The problem is Trump squatted on that space instead.</div> </div> </div> </div> I have heard the republicans are masters of Gerrymandering.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T09:24:14-08:00","id":1002545,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1000616-2016-election-result-thread?p=1002494#post1002494\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Again, you keep asserting that the marginalized motivation for Trump supporters was specifically anti-Democratic. What's your evidence on this? Everything I've seen suggests that it was not motivated by specific party positions and policies over general dissatisfaction with the establishment. The Parties of the nominees was incidental to the motivation of why they voted.</div> </div> </div> </div>You don't think that various policies, legislation and positions supported and pushed by Democrats like NAFTA, the TTP, or Obamacare had anything to do with why some people voted for Trump over Hillary?<br /> <br /> Personally I think those had a lot to do with the way quite a few people voted. I think Hillary did so poorly with northern union voters compared to previous candidates in states like Pennsylvania because of Democratic created or supported policies and positions that many voters felt were hurting them personally. And I think that losing those voters is a big factor in why she lots the election. If those union voters had voted for Hillary at the same levels they have voted for other Democratic presidential candidates, she would have taken Pennsylvania and Wisconsin at least.<br /> <br /> Writing off all of Trump's voters by saying, &quot;They didn't vote because of the positions of the two parties, they only voted for Trump because they were angry,&quot; paints with a pretty poor brush. Many of them <i>were</i> angry, but they were angry specifically because of Democratic policies and initiatives such as Obamacare and various free trade legislation. Hopefully Democrats do a better job of realizing why they lost the election than simple &quot;anger&quot; at the establishment.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T11:22:15-08:00","id":1002570,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1000616-2016-election-result-thread?p=1002535#post1002535\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have heard the republicans are masters of Gerrymandering.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Democrats are just as good at it. The Republicans have just had a significant advantage because of the way things like the House and the EC give small population states much more proportionate power. By having a smaller core base, but spread out over more states and more districts that have highly valued voters, they can entrench themselves rather thoroughly to guard against anti-Gerrymandering or Democratic Gerrymandering.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1000616-2016-election-result-thread?p=1002545#post1002545\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You don't think that various policies, legislation and positions supported and pushed by Democrats like NAFTA, the TTP, or Obamacare had anything to do with why some people voted for Trump over Hillary?</div> </div> </div> </div>I didn't say that. I was challenging the assertion that it was the primary motivation of those voters. You're the one that very much pushing the &quot;perception&quot; angle of this, and the perceptions I hear (btw, I live in rural PA... so however anecdotal I hear all of this, and it's the same thing I hear in pretty much every journalistic attempt to ask this voting group about why they voted), are not, &quot;We voted for Trump because of Democratic policies.&quot; Obamacare is controversial, but not a wedge issue here. Traditionally Democratic voters generally support the majority of the law. Trump's insistence that he'll replace it with something better might have helped them overcome that, but if he was running purely on &quot;toss Obamacare in the trash and go back to how it was,&quot; it would have hurt Trump significantly with the same voter bloc.<br /> <br /> Trump's anti-policy campaign made this debate really difficult. &quot;Here is a family you know, that's out of debt and their kid is alive because of Obamacare, and tossing them off is going to screw them,&quot; just gets countered with, &quot;well we trust Trump's plan for health care is going to replace all that stuff and just fix the bad stuff instead.&quot; And there's was no ability to dig into that debate because there was no Trump healthcare policy paper to discuss if that was really true or not.<br /> <br /> You seem to be significantly underestimating how much of the white blue-collar voting bloc doesn't seem to like either party all that much. A lot of polling suggests there's a significant, &quot;both parties are equally bad,&quot; sentiment because they see things like trade deals as both parties fault. Which is actually a fair accusation, trade deals are rarely the priority for Democratic supermajorities, because they can trust there's enough pro-trade deal Republicans to get them passed later, and the Republican establishment tends to be just happy using trade deals as bipartisan stuff when they're in power.<br /> <br /> Again, all the attempts to dig into this find, &quot;the government screwed us over, so lets put in the not-government guy,&quot; is a much stronger motivation than, &quot;the Democrats screwed us over, lets vote against Hillary (or even generic Democrat).&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Personally I think those had a lot to do with the way quite a few people voted. </div> </div> </div>OK. Based on what evidence? Where's the actual evidence for this impression of the perception of the election?<br /> <br /> Again, most people think of things like NAFTA as &quot;both party's fault,&quot; because that's actually true. Bush Sr. was the one that negotiated NAFTA. Clinton signed it. More Republicans in the Congress voted for it than Democrats. Trying to hang NAFTA solely on the necks of the Democrats is factually incorrect; which is why that's not what Trump's campaign did. He hung it on the necks of the establishment because that's true. The Republican and Democrat establishments made NAFTA happen together; if either party had united against it, it would have died.<br /> <br /> Edit: Also again, go back to the primary. Jeb had a huge problem getting any traction, part of that is because the &quot;stink&quot; of NAFTA is all over his last name too. Bush Jr. signed 9 free-trade deals into law as well (we only have 14 active ones at the moment...). Trump used anti-free trade rhetoric a lot in the primary because it sticks to both sides.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And I think that losing those voters is a big factor in why she lots the election. If those union voters had voted for Hillary at the same levels they have voted for other Democratic presidential candidates, she would have taken Pennsylvania and Wisconsin at least. </div> </div> </div>Absolutely. I don't think anyone is questioning that. Hell, she didn't even need them to vote anywhere near Obama numbers. 100K more votes in each of the core Rust Belt states would have been enough for her to win the whole election (that's how close Pennsylvania, Michigan, and Wisconsin were, 100K votes more in each and we'd have president election Clinton). Not a huge margin of victory, but enough. My only contention with the phrasing is that the &quot;union&quot; part doesn't really apply any more, because the industrial unions are basically dead in rural America.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Writing off all of Trump's voters... </div> </div> </div>I'm not. I'm disregarding anyone that wasn't in the Republican camp anyway, or the people that normally don't vote but voted for Trump, and so on. I'm talking just about the normally Democratic leaning blue collar worker voting bloc. I'm also looking at what those voters are saying (wasn't that the whole problem, that nobody but Trump seemed to actually be listening to them?), and it's not what you're saying.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":617,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","user":"Heavy Arms","date":". 11-13-2016, 02:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T11:27:01-08:00","id":1002572,"title":"","content":"I'm just tired of people thinking Florida is part of the same blob of red at the bottom of the map. MOST of Florida is moderate, with the panhandle(the part that borders Alabama.) being very conservative, and Miami being very liberal. That said, I think maybe it's time for us to make it clear that Florida is NOT a red state. Let Alabama keep it's rednecks, Central and South Florida would be better off without them. I say we redraw the map, so Tallahassee is no longer part of Florida! \u200bLet's make Orlando our capital!<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:72px\"><span style=\"color:#0000CD\">#Flexit</span></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/12.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div> &quot;A free society is one in which it is safe to be unpopular.&quot;<br /> <span style=\"color:DarkRed\"><span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Comic Sans MS\">I find <a href=\"https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&amp;rct=j&amp;q=&amp;esrc=s&amp;source=web&amp;cd=3&amp;cad=rja&amp;uact=8&amp;ved=0ahUKEwin4NXQzLfXAhVGySYKHV3TDXYQFgg3MAI&amp;url=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.ign.com%2Farticles%2F2017%2F04%2F25%2Fmarvels-nick-spencer-on-captain-americas-betrayal-and-handling-fan-reactions&amp;usg=AOvVaw2EcsX7n5ebntygb0TizyGl\" target=\"_blank\">Nick Spencer</a> and <a href=\"https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&amp;rct=j&amp;q=&amp;esrc=s&amp;source=web&amp;cd=4&amp;cad=rja&amp;uact=8&amp;ved=0ahUKEwiK7d7szLfXAhXCWSYKHTpKBbcQFgg4MAM&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fen.wikipedia.org%2Fwiki%2FRichard_B._Spencer&amp;usg=AOvVaw1tuP6phfINia-bh_jWsDT_\" target=\"_blank\">Richard Spencer</a> to be equally repulsive.</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6138-mangle77","name":"Mangle77","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6138_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T11:38:40-08:00","id":1002582,"title":"","content":"Dividing Florida up has come up a few times. Another aspect of it is similar to the thing that happened in Colorado, there's just such a difference between the south and the north and the south has to abide by the decisions of people who live in a completely different environment. And Tallahassee and the panhandle don't have anything to worry about regarding rising tides and ocean waters. Miami and other cities have it as an imminent problem.<br /> <br /> I don't really see it happening but some sort of solution would be nice.<br /> <br /> Need to make a sweep and pick up the Greater Antilles. Have a nice Caribbean League going on.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 11-13-2016, 02:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T12:13:54-08:00","id":1002596,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1000616-2016-election-result-thread?p=1002570#post1002570\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Again, all the attempts to dig into this find, &quot;the government screwed us over, so lets put in the not-government guy,&quot; is a much stronger motivation than, &quot;the Democrats screwed us over, lets vote against Hillary (or even generic Democrat).&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>So you don't think that specific policies passed and supported by Democrats like NAFTA or Obamacare, policies that Trump (and many others across the spectrum) attacked as the very thing that was hurting those blue collar workers, had much of an impact on people voting for Trump and against the Democrats? At the least, I think the Democrats have been the party that was tarred the hardest with the free-trade brush, and some of the things Hillary said (like calling the TTP the gold standard of trade agreements, or talking about her dream of a hemispheric common market with free trade and open borders) really hurt her standing with certain segments of the population, especially when you look at certain Democratic policies that have supported those kinds of ideas.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T12:24:23-08:00","id":1002599,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mangle77</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1000616-2016-election-result-thread?p=1002572#post1002572\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm just tired of people thinking Florida is part of the same blob of red at the bottom of the map. MOST of Florida is moderate, with the panhandle(the part that borders Alabama.) being very conservative, and Miami being very liberal. That said, I think maybe it's time for us to make it clear that Florida is NOT a red state. Let Alabama keep it's rednecks, Central and South Florida would be better off without them. I say we redraw the map, so Tallahassee is no longer part of Florida! \u200bLet's make Orlando our capital!</div> </div> </div> </div>I have been to Florida a few times over the last several decades and I have to say that even just from driving through the state I've been able to see some major changes in population demographics. To my understanding, from speaking with people who live in the state, those changes are primarily tied to two things.<br /> <br /> First is White Flight, with many whites leaving the southern sections of the state and resettling in the northern portions due to rising minority population levels in those southern parts of the state. White Flight isn't terribly uncommon in many places, but in Florida rather than going to suburbs outside of the city, many people in Florida are choosing to resettle in a different part of the state - specifically the northern sections in the panhandle and near the Georgia border, which has helped make Jacksonville the only major city in Florida that leans conservative.<br /> <br /> The other factor is that Florida is, of course, still a fairly popular place for retirees to move to, and many of them tend to settle in the northern sections of the state (and again, most elderly people tend to be conservative).<br /> <br /> While many states have a &quot;split&quot; between liberal sections and conservative sections, the geography of such a split is a bit more evident in Florida than many other states, though you can see similar geo-political lines in states like California, with many conservatives living in the north, or Colorado where liberals tend to be clustered in the center of the state and surrounded by a red wall of conservative rural areas on all sides.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T13:06:22-08:00","id":1002616,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1000616-2016-election-result-thread?p=1002582#post1002582\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't really see it happening but some sort of solution would be nice.</div> </div> </div> </div>A solution is something the country needs in general. Florida is a somewhat unique example in terms of being able to draw a neat-ish line between left and right leaning parts of the state (though you can do that for Washington and Oregon as well), but the overall effect is evident throughout the country. Our system really was not designed to handle the amount of clustering of populations in urban centers that's the continuing trend for so long now. The structures that were supposed to protect the minority from the majority have done far more to enable the minority over-ruling the majority (in many aspects beyond political affiliation, and by both sides thereof).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1000616-2016-election-result-thread?p=1002596#post1002596\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So you don't think that specific policies passed and supported by Democrats like NAFTA or Obamacare, policies that Trump (and many others across the spectrum) attacked as the very thing that was hurting those blue collar workers, had much of an impact on people voting for Trump and against the Democrats?</div> </div> </div> </div>Can you stop constructing strawmen?<br /> <br /> I didn't say that. I said that the <b>perception</b> of the traditionally Democratic voters that either didn't vote or went Trump - which was one of many things that if prevented would have made Clinton president elect - is not motivated by linking party and policy.<br /> <br /> &quot;I can't vote Democrat any more because they backed stuff like NAFTA,&quot; is virtually non-existent in that cohort compared to, &quot;I can't vote for establishment types because they all pass this stuff.&quot;<br /> <br /> And, again, Obamacare is not a wedge issue here. People want a functional ACA in this voting cohort. The broken parts of the ACA remaining unfixed and hurting people isn't a Democrat problem, it's a dysfunctional government problem that all establishment painted individuals get hit with by this demographic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> At the least, I think the Democrats have been the party that was tarred the hardest with the free-trade brush, and some of the things Hillary said (like calling the TTP the gold standard of trade agreements, or talking about her dream of a hemispheric common market with free trade and open borders) really hurt her standing with certain segments of the population, especially when you look at certain Democratic policies that have supported those kinds of ideas. </div> </div> </div>Eh. Again, I point to the primaries. The establishment Republicans got tarred with this plenty. The only way the Republicans didn't get it worse is that they capitulated and became the party of Trump.<br /> <br /> OK. To try to get this somewhere:<br /> <br /> 1) Are you actually trying to claim that free-trade is factually something that is more a Democratic policy tradition, or just trying to speak about perceptions? Because you keep talking about free-trade as a purely Democratic issue despite the vast, vast, vast evidence to the contrary, and do so in fashions that don't seem to just apply to the discussion of perceptions.<br /> <br /> 2) What are you basing your impressions about these perceptions on? Talking to people on the ground? Polls? Journalists? What? You keep saying things like, &quot;I think the...&quot; but you don't say where that thought is coming from. It feels a lot like you're trying to just reverse engineer this; that you feel Democratic policies are the problem, and then shoehorn this voting bloc's motivations into that basic assumption that the core issue is Democratic policies.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T14:11:10-08:00","id":1002634,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1000616-2016-election-result-thread?p=1002616#post1002616\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I didn't say that. I said that the <b>perception</b> of the traditionally Democratic voters that either didn't vote or went Trump - which was one of many things that if prevented would have made Clinton president elect - is not motivated by linking party and policy.<br /> <br /> &quot;I can't vote Democrat any more because they backed stuff like NAFTA,&quot; is virtually non-existent in that cohort compared to, &quot;I can't vote for establishment types because they all pass this stuff.&quot;<br /> <br /> And, again, Obamacare is not a wedge issue here. People want a functional ACA in this voting cohort. The broken parts of the ACA remaining unfixed and hurting people isn't a Democrat problem, it's a dysfunctional government problem that all establishment painted individuals get hit with by this demographic.</div> </div> </div> </div>And I disagree. I think Hillary's stances (and the Democrat's stances) on a lot of issues were responsible for a lot of Trump's votes. Many people in Rustbelt states have felt forgotten by the Democratic party and voted with Trump because he was willing to talk about them, their problems and discussed the issues that they felt strongly about - issues that have often been ignored by other candidates.<br /> <br /> Many traditionally Democratic voters voted against Hillary not <i>only</i> because they were angry and emotional - rather a record number turned out for Trump because quite a few have felt ignored, used, abused and left behind by Democratic initiatives and legislation. Trump came out swinging against many of those like NAFTA and other policies which Hillary, Obama and the Democrats have supportered; policies which quite a few people hold as responsible for the problems that they have faced in their own day-to-day lives. They were angry and upset specifically with Democrats (and Hillary) because of the policies enacted by Democrats (including her husband). <br /> <br /> If you're trying to argue that the legislation and initiatives passed or lobbied for by Democrats over the past two-dozen years, such as NAFTA, Obamacare, the TTP, etc, had no perceptible effect on the election, I would just have to flatly disagree. Those were all important bits and pieces that helped push people over the hump and into Trump's camp rather than staying on the Democrat's camp where they spent the last 20 or 30 years.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T14:26:37-08:00","id":1002640,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1000616-2016-election-result-thread?p=1002467#post1002467\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hopefully however the Democrats take things pretty seriously, learn from their mistakes and ramp up their outreach and attempts to connect with voters who felt marginalized by Democratic positions and policies to the point that many of them decided to vote for Trump...</div> </div> </div> </div>Regardless of why people voted from Trump, I think it would be even more disastrous for the nation if the &quot;lesson&quot; the Democrats learn from this is that they need to run Honey-Boo-Boo and adopt goofy positions. Pretty much everything that Trump ran on was flatly absurd -- and yet huge swaths of the electorate voted for him anyway (even though his own party didn't really want him). I'd frankly rather the Democrats continue to lose elections than have them cave in to the growing arrogant-ignorance of the American electorate and start adopting policy positions that they <i>know</i> make no sense.<br /> <br /> \u200bClinton was pro-TPP. Not because she hates working-class Americans but because she knew that (despite its many flaws) it was the best deal the U.S. could get. Backing away from it was stupid because everybody <i>knew</i> she was for it, and flipping like that just made her seem more &quot;shifty&quot;. She should have stuck to her guns and if it didn't resonate with voters, then they could deal with a Trump Administration (which is what wound up happening anyway). Just because something isn't popular, doesn't mean it's not the right move. David Brooks of the New York Times got it absolutely right: some things are more important than winning elections.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/546/wpur.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/545/7z8m.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3506-uniform-two-six","name":"Uniform Two Six","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3506_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"455"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T15:07:52-08:00","id":1002653,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1000616-2016-election-result-thread?p=1002545#post1002545\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You don't think that various policies, legislation and positions supported and pushed by Democrats like NAFTA, the TTP, or Obamacare had anything to do with why some people voted for Trump over Hillary?<br /> <br /> Personally I think those had a lot to do with the way quite a few people voted. I think Hillary did so poorly with northern union voters compared to previous candidates in states like Pennsylvania because of Democratic created or supported policies and positions that many voters felt were hurting them personally. And I think that losing those voters is a big factor in why she lots the election. If those union voters had voted for Hillary at the same levels they have voted for other Democratic presidential candidates, she would have taken Pennsylvania and Wisconsin at least.<br /> <br /> Writing off all of Trump's voters by saying, &amp;quot;They didn't vote because of the positions of the two parties, they only voted for Trump because they were angry,&amp;quot; paints with a pretty poor brush. Many of them <i>were</i> angry, but they were angry specifically because of Democratic policies and initiatives such as Obamacare and various free trade legislation. Hopefully Democrats do a better job of realizing why they lost the election than simple &amp;quot;anger&amp;quot; at the establishment.</div> </div> </div> </div> Anyone who thinks that its had nothing to do with various actions and policies of the democractic party and its leadership are kidding themselves.<br /> <br /> That's why Tim Ryan is gunning for Nancy Polosi's job as minority leader, or at least that's what I'm hearing.<br /> <br /> The Young Turks and others, mostly Bernie supporters, but also others who felt let down by Hillary, and others who are frustracted, are going to take the fight now not just to Trump, but to the DNC, cleaning house and setting things up for a candiate who will focus on economic policies they support, the policies of Bernie Sanders and simular people. This election was a revolution in a way, but its only the first, the next is going go happen in the democractic party.<br /> <br /> Its the beginning of the end of the neoliberal nightmare.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T15:57:00-08:00","id":1002675,"title":"","content":"I just my queer brothers and sisters are safe for the next four years.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T16:10:05-08:00","id":1002684,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Uniform Two Six</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1000616-2016-election-result-thread?p=1002640#post1002640\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd frankly rather the Democrats continue to lose elections than have them cave in to the growing arrogant-ignorance of the American electorate and start adopting policy positions that they <i>know</i> make no sense.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm sure Republicans and conservatives would rather see that happen too. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T16:19:36-08:00","id":1002689,"title":"","content":"I was raised as one of Jehovah's Witnesses. The last time a loudmouth with a bad comb-over became leader of a country by vilifying minorities and promising to return the nation's glory days, <a href=\"http://wol.jw.org/en/wol/d/r1/lp-e/1200274702\" target=\"_blank\">things got really bad for us...</a>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/12.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div> &quot;A free society is one in which it is safe to be unpopular.&quot;<br /> <span style=\"color:DarkRed\"><span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Comic Sans MS\">I find <a href=\"https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&amp;rct=j&amp;q=&amp;esrc=s&amp;source=web&amp;cd=3&amp;cad=rja&amp;uact=8&amp;ved=0ahUKEwin4NXQzLfXAhVGySYKHV3TDXYQFgg3MAI&amp;url=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.ign.com%2Farticles%2F2017%2F04%2F25%2Fmarvels-nick-spencer-on-captain-americas-betrayal-and-handling-fan-reactions&amp;usg=AOvVaw2EcsX7n5ebntygb0TizyGl\" target=\"_blank\">Nick Spencer</a> and <a href=\"https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&amp;rct=j&amp;q=&amp;esrc=s&amp;source=web&amp;cd=4&amp;cad=rja&amp;uact=8&amp;ved=0ahUKEwiK7d7szLfXAhXCWSYKHTpKBbcQFgg4MAM&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fen.wikipedia.org%2Fwiki%2FRichard_B._Spencer&amp;usg=AOvVaw1tuP6phfINia-bh_jWsDT_\" target=\"_blank\">Richard Spencer</a> to be equally repulsive.</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6138-mangle77","name":"Mangle77","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6138_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T18:44:44-08:00","id":1002734,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1000616-2016-election-result-thread?p=1002634#post1002634\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And I disagree.</div> </div> </div> </div>Clearly. But as far as I can tell, you're being just as guilty as the Republicans and Democrats that tried to stop Trump regarding these people: you're not listening to them.<br /> <br /> You're saying what they believe for them, not actually listening to what they care about and how that impacted their votes this year. All you're managing to say is that because they didn't turn out for Hillary well enough for her to win, whatever reasoning you want to assign must be the case. I directly asked you to at least explain where you're getting this impression - not cite sources, just what kind of sources you're listening to - and you've ignored it.<br /> <br /> Your mind doesn't know what Rust Belt voters think is you're not listening to them, and as the establishment of both parties was forced to confront this year, that's not how good decisions about why they vote are made.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Many traditionally Democratic voters voted against Hillary not <i>only</i> because they were angry and emotional - rather a record number turned out for Trump because quite a few have felt ignored, used, abused and left behind by Democratic initiatives and legislation. </div> </div> </div>You realize my posts in this whole section have not actually asserted anger at all, right?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Trump came out swinging against many of those like NAFTA and other policies which Hillary, Obama and the Democrats have supportered; policies which quite a few people hold as responsible for the problems that they have faced in their own day-to-day lives. They were angry and upset specifically with Democrats (and Hillary) because of the policies enacted by Democrats (including her husband). </div> </div> </div>And clearly you think Rust Belt voters are too stupid to remember how much the Republican party supported NAFTA, because they do, and that's why they blame the political establishment as a whole over just one party.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If you're trying to argue that the legislation and initiatives passed or lobbied for by Democrats over the past two-dozen years, such as NAFTA, Obamacare, the TTP, etc, had no perceptible effect on the election, I would just have to flatly disagree. </div> </div> </div>I'm not, so you can stop talking about things I'm not saying at any point and actually try to engage with what I'm saying.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T19:11:37-08:00","id":1002745,"title":"","content":"Trump just put a man who has called for ethnic cleansings on his team. And so far all I've been seeing all over my social media has boiled down to this. &quot;Mexicans are rapists, Muslims are all a threat, I like to sexually assault women! &quot;I'm don't want to be sexually assaulted, I don't want to be harmed and targeted because I'm LGBT, black,brown, Muslim,etc.&quot; &quot;OMG BOTH SIDES GUYS!&quot;<br /> What a brave new post fact world we are in.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T19:31:50-08:00","id":1002747,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1000616-2016-election-result-thread?p=1002734#post1002734\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Clearly. But as far as I can tell, you're being just as guilty as the Republicans and Democrats that tried to stop Trump regarding these people: you're not listening to them.<br /> <br /> You're saying what they believe for them, not actually listening to what they care about and how that impacted their votes this year. All you're managing to say is that because they didn't turn out for Hillary well enough for her to win, whatever reasoning you want to assign must be the case. I directly asked you to at least explain where you're getting this impression - not cite sources, just what kind of sources you're listening to - and you've ignored it.<br /> <br /> Your mind doesn't know what Rust Belt voters think is you're not listening to them, and as the establishment of both parties was forced to confront this year, that's not how good decisions about why they vote are made.</div> </div> </div> </div>Pretty much everything that's been written about the Rust Belt area in this campaign and previous campaigns. The fact is, the Rust Belt has been slowly leaning more red in each election over the last 20 years. That's not a coincidence, and it's not because the Democrats have been in charge of everything for the last 20 years. A big part of the reason for that is that many people in that part of the world feel that Democratic policies simply don't take them and their part of America into account - that Democrats take their votes for granted and then turn around and and ignore them or worse, promote policies that actively hurt them.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.washingtonpost.com/posteverything/wp/2016/11/09/the-rust-belt-was-turning-red-already-donald-trump-just-pushed-it-along/\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.washingtonpost.com/poste...shed-it-along/</a><br /> <br /> I mean, there's tons of similar articles that show that the Rust Belt has been leaning more and more red every year and more and more union workers are turning Republican out of frustration with Democrats and their policies. Trump grabbed far more union votes than any other Republican candidate has done so in a long time, and the union votes that Hillary lost and went to Trump were pretty much what put him ahead in so many states that have been traditionally Democrat.<br /> <br /> So the question is, why did a record number of union voters decide to break for the Republican candidate over the Democratic one? I think to a degree it was natural - Republicans have slowly seen more and more union voters in past elections but nothing like this one. Trump simply harnessed anger at Democrats in general thanks to many of the specific Democratic policies (like NAFTA and other free trade agreements) that many union workers feel hurt them.<br /> <br /> In the end, he was able to tar Hillary and the Democrats as the free-trade candidates (helped in large part by things Hillary has said at various times) and she was never able to try and remind people that the Republicans are pretty free trade too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":741,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","user":"AnubisXy","date":". 11-14-2016, 12:00 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T20:47:01-08:00","id":1002758,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TyrannicalRabbit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1000616-2016-election-result-thread?p=1002745#post1002745\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Trump just put a man who has called for ethnic cleansings on his team. And so far all I've been seeing all over my social media has boiled down to this. &quot;Mexicans are rapists, Muslims are all a threat, I like to sexually assault women! &quot;I'm don't want to be sexually assaulted, I don't want to be harmed and targeted because I'm LGBT, black,brown, Muslim,etc.&quot; &quot;OMG BOTH SIDES GUYS!&quot;<br /> What a brave new post fact world we are in.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> ...&quot;New&quot;?<br /> <br /> What really gets me, I think, is the assumption that dodging the question like this is something new. It ascribes to Trump something (in)auspicious that he really doesn't deserve, rather than being some schmuck who lucked out after a comedy-roast induced temper tantrum.<br /> <br /> There has always been people who omit facts for the sake of ideals. Many of them were on our side-including me.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T21:22:20-08:00","id":1002763,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1000616-2016-election-result-thread?p=1002490#post1002490\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Agreed. The Republicans have nothing to crow about, Trump hijacked their party, its now the Trump party effectively, not the party of Reagan anymore.</div> </div> </div> </div>We'll see about that. The battle for the heart of the Republican party isn't over by a long shot; in many ways, it's just beginning.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T21:32:03-08:00","id":1002764,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leliel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1000616-2016-election-result-thread?p=1002758#post1002758\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> ...&quot;New&quot;?<br /> <br /> What really gets me, I think, is the assumption that dodging the question like this is something new. It ascribes to Trump something (in)auspicious that he really doesn't deserve, rather than being some schmuck who lucked out after a comedy-roast induced temper tantrum.<br /> <br /> There has always been people who omit facts for the sake of ideals. Many of them were on our side-including me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Or it could be a reference to the way that it's been turned front and center this election rather than saying that it originated with this election. I suggest you learn the difference.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T21:34:55-08:00","id":1002765,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TyrannicalRabbit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1000616-2016-election-result-thread?p=1002764#post1002764\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Or it could be a reference to the way that it's been turned front and center this election rather than saying that it originated with this election. I suggest you learn the difference.</div> </div> </div> </div>I do know the difference. That was what I was saying.<br /> <br /> I was not aware you were saying that. My bad.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T22:16:09-08:00","id":1002770,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1000616-2016-election-result-thread?p=1002747#post1002747\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Pretty much everything that's been written about the Rust Belt area in this campaign and previous campaigns.</div> </div> </div> </div>So... basically you're saying that you don't actually bother with sources and are just asserting this because you can?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The fact is, the Rust Belt has been slowly leaning more red in each election over the last 20 years. </div> </div> </div>Um.... not really. Lets take PA for example. Presidential numbers were steadily getting better until this election and the last one (but not a huge dip there). The House was nearly constant with redistricting being the only reason PA Congressmen don't get a steady rise, until 2010. So... where's this slowly going more red evidence?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <a href=\"https://www.washingtonpost.com/posteverything/wp/2016/11/09/the-rust-belt-was-turning-red-already-donald-trump-just-pushed-it-along/\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.washingtonpost.com/poste...shed-it-along/</a> </div> </div> </div>Nothing in this article provides a single point of data that the results of this election were predicated on animus aimed at the Democrats and linking specific policies to the Democrats. There's no, &quot;We don't like the Democrat's NAFTA,&quot; just, &quot;We don't like NAFTA.&quot; And again, a lot of people in the Rust Belt remember how much the Republicans were responsible for NAFTA too.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Trump simply harnessed anger at Democrats in general thanks to many of the specific Democratic policies (like NAFTA and other free trade agreements) that many union workers feel hurt them. </div> </div> </div>So, again you're saying you don't actually care about what these people are saying, you just want to project your opinions on them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In the end, he was able to tar Hillary and the Democrats as the free-trade candidates (helped in large part by things Hillary has said at various times) and she was never able to try and remind people that the Republicans are pretty free trade too. </div> </div> </div>This is most true, but that's the point here. He had to tar them as as the free-trade group, because he recognized anyone associated with free trade would be hurt by it; not because he was tapping into anger specifically already aimed at the Democrats looking for a voice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-14T04:51:51-08:00","id":1002833,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://m.huffpost.com/us/entry/us_5828a7c9e4b060adb56ee9fd\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://img.huffingtonpost.com/asset/5828abd0180000ca0330dbe4.jpeg?cache=jrPsPbIGGM&amp;ops=1200_630\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://m.huffpost.com/us/entry/us_5828a7c9e4b060adb56ee9fd\" target=\"_blank\"> This Is Who Should Challenge Nancy Pelosi For Leadership Of The Democratic Party </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://m.huffpost.com/us/entry/us_5828a7c9e4b060adb56ee9fd\" target=\"_blank\"> http://m.huffpost.com/us/entry/us_5828a7c9e4b060adb56ee9fd </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> This Is Who Should Challenge Nancy Pelosi For Leadership Of The Democratic Party </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Tim Ryan 